  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nHigh pressure has began to weaken across the Desert Southwest.\nThis will begin to bring subtle increases in cooling and onshore \nflow over the next few days. Temperatures will not be as hot for \nareas west of the mountains this afternoon, though the desert \nregions will be similar to yesterday. Onshore winds will slightly \nstronger across the mountain desert slopes and adjacent deserts \nlater this afternoon and evening nearing 25-40 MPH. These winds will \noccur again on Monday. The marine layer will remain thinner, with \npatchy clouds and fog closer to the coast each morning.\n\nA few degrees of cooling can be expected into the middle of the week \nas subtle changes in high's movement occur. Temperatures will still \nremain on the warmer side with highs slightly above normal for \ninland valleys, mountains, and deserts with areas of moderate heat \nrisk. Models indicate an increase in monsoonal moisture moving into \nthe area during this time period as well with the largest increase \nexpected on Wednesday, so we can expect more humid conditions by \nthis time onward. Cumulus clouds are likely to form over the \nmountains each afternoon starting Wednesday through later in the \nweek. Chances for rainfall still look minimal near 20% or less each \nday over the higher terrain. Other areas look to see highs near to \nslightly above normal with dry weather.",
